WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Welcome to Grade 8 English, Unit
6: Natural Wonders and Conservation.

2
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:13.540
In this unit, you will explore the extraordinary landscapes
of East Africa and beyond, discovering how mountains,

3
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.320
valleys, rivers, and wildlife shape the world around us.

4
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:24.820
You will read an original adventure story called "The Citadel
of Wind and Stone," set in the dramatic highlands of the

5
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:25.580
Horn of Africa,

6
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:32.260
where a group of young people must work together, make
difficult decisions, and learn the meaning of true leadership.

7
00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:38.800
Through this story, you will encounter powerful
vocabulary, practise important grammar structures,

8
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.660
and develop your speaking and writing skills.

9
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.380
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:55.000
By the end of this unit, you will be able to use
comparative and superlative forms of adjectives correctly,

11
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.420
including irregular forms like better, worse, best, and worst.

12
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.860
You will also form and use comparative adverb
structures to compare actions (for example,

13
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.800
"She completed the task more quickly than he did").

14
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.880
You will also understand and use pronouns
and quantifiers such as anyone, nothing, few,

15
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.700
and all in your writing and speech.

16
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.000
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

17
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:28.480
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: formalisation,
fragmentation, biosphere, ecosystem-level, trophic,

18
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:29.440
tectonics.

19
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:35.060
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

20
00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.720
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

21
00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:45.780
The reading sequence includes The Roof of Africa:
Wonders Worth Keeping and Should the High Trails Close?

22
00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:48.380
Two Voices from the Reserve.

23
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.360
Read actively.

24
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:55.340
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

25
00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.500
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

26
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.900
The first language focus is Forming
Comparatives with -er and More.

27
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:13.260
Comparatives weigh two things against each other: short
adjectives add -er, longer adjectives take more in front,

28
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:19.480
and than introduces the second item, as in 'The
mountain trail is steeper than the coastal path.'

29
00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.820
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

30
00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:33.360
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Comparing Natural Wonders.

31
00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.400
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

32
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.780
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

33
00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.280
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

34
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:46.900
You are ready to begin.

35
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:53.440
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

36
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.080
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

37
00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:03.720
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
